WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:05.470
How characters should've looked according
to comics

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.389
Comic book movie costumes are getting more
and more accurate with every film that's released.

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:15.199
The outfit featured in Deadpool set the new
standard, and both Black Panther and Spider-Man's

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:21.620
costumes in Captain America: Civil War look
fantastic. But for every comic-accurate costume,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.710
there are plenty more page-to-screen adaptations
that are just...wrong.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.330
Sometimes ignoring original designs is a smart
decision, while other times it's hard not

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.210
to be bummed about the changes made for the
sake of "realism." Here are some of the most

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.960
off-kilter attempts to put super-suits on
the big screen from the past, present, and

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.460
future…

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.560
Wolverine

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.600
Back when Hugh Jackman first took the role
of Wolverine in 2000's X-Men, it was impressive

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.579
enough that he accurately captured the essence
of everyone's favorite clawed psychopath.

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:59.380
It didn't much matter that, at 6'2", Jackman
stands a full 11 inches taller than his super-short

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.530
comic book counterpart. But despite the fact
that fans have loved the movie version of

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.990
Wolverine for over a decade, he's still never
appeared on-screen in his comic-accurate costume.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.709
C'mon, can't we even get the mask…

00:01:11.709 --> 00:01:14.350
...on his face? Just once?

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:14.889
Vibe

00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:20.200
We can't really blame the producers of the
CW's The Flash for not going so comic book

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.380
accurate with this one. For one thing, Cisco
Ramon, aka Vibe, is still discovering his

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.599
powers on the show, and hasn't gone out to
fight crime as a full-on superhero. Moreover,

00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:35.209
until a recent redesign, Vibe's comic book
suit was pretty embarrassing, with its red

00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:39.869
handkerchief and V-neck collar that goes all
the way down. Still, if the show keeps looking

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:45.389
to score points for comic book accuracy, we
have to point out that it's missed this one—even

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:48.649
when it makes the smart choice by actually
avoiding it.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:49.270
Hawkeye

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.590
This is another instance where the filmmakers
behind Hawkeye's big screen look made the

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:59.609
right move—by moving away from the comics.
Sure, Hawkeye's all-purple archery suit looks

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:05.169
cool on the page, but it'd be hard not to
make it look ridiculous at the movie theater.

00:02:05.169 --> 00:02:06.009
Superman

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:11.080
Lots of Superman haters have pointed to his
costume as something in need of "fixing."

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.950
With the yellow belt that holds nothing up,
the red underpants on the outside, and the

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:19.750
basic blue spandex, it's definitely a simple
outfit, and one that hasn't changed much over

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.170
the past century.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.860
But Christopher Reeve managed to make it look
awesome in 1978's Superman, and all three

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.450
of the sequels. That was proof enough that
Superman's costume wasn't actually broken

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:30.930
at all.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.330
Yet, for some reason, Zack Snyder insisted
on making Superman's belt even weirder—and

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:42.710
cover him in fish-scales?—for 2013's Man
of Steel. Bring the underpants back, man.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.580
"Nnnoooooooooo!"

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:47.400
Deadpool

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.080
Much has already been said about how badly
the film's producers screwed up the character:

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.850
they take him out of his iconic costume, sew
his mouth shut, and give him all of the mutant

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.770
powers they could stuff into one person.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:04.390
The good news, of course, is that it paved
the way for 2016's Deadpool, which has broken

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:11.320
new ground for faithful comic book adaptations...and
for creative use of a unicorn doll in an R-rated

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.000
movie.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:12.370
Punisher

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.720
While it's not going to win any awards for
being clever, the Punisher's costume is pretty

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.390
memorable for exactly one reason: it has a
gigantic white skull on it. So when the Dolph

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.270
Lundgren-starring Punisher movie came out
in 1989, what's the one thing the filmmakers

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:32.700
decided to omit from the character's look?
The gigantic white skull. Fortunately, all

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.950
the on-screen Punishers since then have let
good sense prevail...and stuck huge, white,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.360
skull-shaped targets on their chests. Phew.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.130
Mystique

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:46.670
As we've discussed, the producers of 2000's
X-Men made some important choices to ensure

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.770
that their team of mutants would look realistic
on film. That meant cutting brightly-colored

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.370
superhero costumes in favor of black leather.
Whether or not that was a more believable

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.810
costume choice is debatable.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.680
But there's no question that director Bryan
Singer's move to change the look of Mystique

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.569
is one of the more baffling adaptations in
superhero movie history. For some reason,

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.630
Singer insisted that the villain be covered
in lizard scales and walk around naked all

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:17.519
day long, despite there being no such corresponding
look in the comics. Stranger still, the scales

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:21.719
have stuck around from movie to movie and
actress to actress, even though it looks truly

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:25.840
bizarre and has no real reason to still be,
like, a thing.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.569
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:35.029
just saw. And leave us a comment to let us
know which comic book costume you want to

00:04:35.029 --> 00:04:35.529
see on film...

